## By machinery

### n-th roots, rational summation, limits (ℝ analysis beyond the ordered field)
- §2.1 Ex 7 — AM–GM for general n (needs n-th roots; the two-variable case
  √(ab) ≤ (a+b)/2 is elementary from `std/real/sqrt.b4m` and not blocked).
- §2.1 Ex 10 — Σ 1/(k(k+1)) = n/(n+1) (needs a ℚ-valued summation surface).
- §2.1 Ex 11 — (1+x)ⁿ − 1 ≥ nx for real x ≥ 0 (Bernoulli; real order + real powers).
- §2.3 Ex 31 (final clause) — √2 ∉ ℚ stated IN ℚ. (The integer core "no p,q with
  p²=2q²" is proved in the §2.3 exercises; the ℚ statement needs a
  numerator/denominator view of `std/rational`.)
- §2.3 Ex 17 (parts c,d) — Fibonacci closed form with √5 and the golden-ratio
  limit (reals + limits).

### Integer summation operator (Σ) + nonlinear polynomial induction
- §2.1 Ex 1 — 1²+2²+···+n² = n(n+1)(2n+1)/6.
- §2.1 Ex 2 — 1³+2³+···+n³ = n²(n+1)²/4.
- §2.1 Ex 4 — x+4x+···+(3n−2)x = n(3n−1)x/2.
  (Each needs a ℤ-native finite-sum Σ and division by a constant; the
  restate-as-`c·Σ = …` form still needs the Σ operator. `std/sequence.b4m` has an
  abstract fold but not an integer-native summation surface these can cite yet.)

### shifted-base / negative-exponent divisibility
- §2.1 Ex 6 — 99 | 4·10^{2n} + 9·10^{2n−1} + 5 (Judson intends n ≥ 1; the
  10^{2n−1} term is 10^{−1} at n=0, so it needs a base-shifted induction).

### perfect-square predicate + gcd-square lemmas
- §2.3 Ex 19 — coprime x,y with xy a perfect square ⟹ x,y both perfect squares
  (needs gcd(a²,b²) = gcd(a,b)² and gcd(ca,cb) = c·gcd(a,b)).

### heavy parity + coprimality case analysis
- §2.3 Ex 21 — pairwise-coprime a,b,r,s with a²+b²=r², a²−b²=s² ⟹ a,r,s odd,
  b even.

### geometric-series divisibility identity over ℤ
- §2.3 Ex 28 — 2^p − 1 prime ⟹ p prime (needs (2^a−1) | (2^{ab}−1)).

### Dirichlet-type prime constructions (residue-class specialization)
- §2.3 Ex 29 — infinitely many primes of the form 6n+5.
- §2.3 Ex 30 — infinitely many primes of the form 4n−1.
  (`std/primes/classes.b4m` has the load-bearing step for both: a number ≡ −1 in a
  residue class above 2 has a prime factor outside the 1 class. What remains is the
  strong induction that iterates it and the Euclid-style construction.)

